r  and I guess if was that always the  intention from the start or how did that  how'd you guys well first of all thank  you for framing the question that way I  wish I could take you I Oliver odd  because you nailed it that's exactly it  it's truly that was I think a  fundamental thing that that that  inspired both Tom and I I think it's  fair to say at the very beginning was  that we wanted to do something totally  different and we wanted to really  elevate the show and a lot of ways and  show that it's while it is indeed a  sketch comedy show and it's it's  hilarious  it's a lot more than a sketch comedy  show and it's become this interesting  barometer in which we can sort of  measure what's happening in our country  at any given time it's this little time  capsule and that notion is what really  appealed to us is that there's been  amazing retrospectives done on saturday  night live for many years there's decade  after decade ken Bowser did a phenomenal  job with those there's an incredible  book there's you know there's just  tremendous materials about the making of  the show and we didn't want to do that  it's been done and it's been done well  so as a sort of respect for those that  came before us we wanted to do something  with a completely fresh perspective I  think while we talked about some  difficult issues it was still respectful  so it was done in a way that you know  again it wasn't about the show per se  and the controversy that surrounded the  show rather it's significant  controversies and issues that are facing  our country in which the show reflected  and I mean certainly if we're talking  about racial diversity it's a very it's  a pretty timely issue in our country and  it's just reflected on the show and I  think it was important to us that we  show you know that it never was you know  it was it was one way this era and now  it's a different way this era it's like  well from 1975 all the rights to 2015  racial diversity has been and is  currently an issue so that's that's the  same with our country and with Saturday  Night Live and I think right you know  all of the topics that you brought up so  I don't think it's in any way sort of an  attack on the show rather it's just  acknowledging a you know a dialogue  that's been there for a long time yeah  now to come back to this bill o'reilly  point that you brought up earlier there  were some people that obviously expect  you're gonna see tina fey or somebody in  this but I giuliani or O'Reilly or some  of these other folks that they appear  would not be obvious choices to somebody  and so I just wonder how you decided who  wasn't and wasn't going to be included  in terms of the talking head portions  yeah I mean I think again it's  definitely a collection of all of our  filmmaking team we had a phenomenal  filmmaking team so we have really strong  producers and a talented director and we  all sat in a room and said you know  let's look at sort of key pivotal  moments in history through this 40-year  window and look at when did we feel as a  collective team that those sort of  historical accent ameri  were reflected on Saturday Night Live so  it wasn't what's the funniest sketch or  what's the most popular you know actor  it was you know what actually resonated  and I think so Giuliana you bring up as  an example you know 911 was obviously a  pivotal moment in our country and how a  comedic show responds to a tragedy like  that is is quite telling and is an  important sort of you know pin point in  history so Giuliani was an integral part  of that and therefore it made sense to  have him and I think for Bill O'Reilly  it was you know a lot of people perceive  it as a liberal show it's entertainment  you know it's a comedy show and we felt  like it was important to have a  conservative voice and and of course  there was the additional benefit of the  fact that he was declined to do the  document or said entertained us but one  question I had for you tom is that  having been there for so long did doing  this examination of the show teach you  anything that you didn't already know  about the way that it it operates or had  you know came to be or anything else  anything that you really learned through  it um no I mean certainly one of the  things I learned you know the whole  creation in the 70s and how the show and  that the germination of how the show  really began and all that sort so and  when asking Lauren some of those  questions some of those questions were  more about well what do I want to know  from him um you know I've read a lot of  stuff about the show that sort of seven  I've spoken to him a lot about the show  but a lot of that was like well you know  what what did I want to know when we  were trying to come up with the  questions now do you think that you  start til you do touch upon the role of  SNL in today's size versus 1975 and I  guess what I wonder what your  conclusions are having really looked at  it under the microscope here is it  what's the state of SNL going forward do  you feel that it can be as culturally  important and relevant in a world in  which there are now instead of three  channels like in 1975 now hundreds  you've got to compete with the internet  which although they have sort of  embraced the way of using that  smartphone all of this is it what's  what's the future look like for them I  mean that's definitely a silver you know  sort of a crystal ball type of question  I I think one of the things that we've  talked about a lot since making the film  is that Saturday Night Live one of the  things that differentiates it from sort  of all of the other programs out there  and makes it such a unique entity is  that it has this really interesting  balance of paying complete and total  respect and credence to its history and  its legacy in its tradition the  structure remains the same the cold open  remains the same you know this history  and branding of the show remains intact  while at the same time every single week  it completely represents what's  happening in the country at that moment  in that current event sort of window  right so it changes with the times but  at the same time stays the same and I  think that's the magic of it and we  addressed a little bit in the  documentary itself about the changing  media landscape and how Saturday Night  Live has changed to accommodate that you  know Andy Samberg spoke at length about  that and I think it just speaks so  strongly of the show that's still today  it is quite you know it is you know in  the sort of forefront it is a  water-cooler show I don't know how many  of you have had this but still to this  day I say if I see something political  happen or I see something pop culture  happen to go I can't wait to see what  they do about this on Saturday and so  that's kind of been ingrained in us and  its generational so while there is  indeed so many more places to get your  content and there's platforms changing  it's really the core as we all know in  the filmmaking community it's the core  of the content that's important and as  long as they keep the let you know  bringing that I think that I think it'll  find its voice so that's our perception  at least you know what I'm having yeah
 

